theorem a-quotient-divisor-said-not-zero
  note        A quotient is a number once its divisor is not zero, which each step writes as a requires line citing step 1.
  let ε ∈ ℝ                                                           (H1)
  let d ∈ ℝ                                                           (H2)
  let n ∈ ℕ                                                           (H3)
  assume ε/(2·d) > 0                                                  (H4)
  assume 1/n > 0                                                      (H5)
  assume d > 0                                                        (H6)
  then 0 − ε/(2·d) < 1/n

1.  2·d ≠ 0
    inequalities
      d > 0                  H6
      2·d > 0                multiplying by 2
      2·d ≠ 0                weakening

2.  0 − ε/(2·d) < 0
    inequalities
      ε/(2·d) > 0            H4
      0 − ε/(2·d) < 0        multiplying by −1
    requires 2·d ≠ 0: from 1

3.  0 − ε/(2·d) < 1/n
    transitivity, from 2, H5
    requires 2·d ≠ 0: from 1
